  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a data structure that evicts the least recently accessed item when its capacity is exceeded. It is widely used in databases, web servers, and operating systems to improve performance by keeping frequently accessed data in fast memory.\n\nPython's `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation for an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order and allows O(1) move-to-end operations via `move_to_end()`. This tutorial walks through a complete, production-quality implementation, explains the semantics of `get` and `put`, covers edge cases, provides tests, and concludes with a worked example and complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## The Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nfrom typing import Any, Hashable, Optional\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded Least Recently Used (LRU) cache.\n\n    The cache has a fixed capacity. When the cache is full and a new key is\n    inserted, the least recently used key is evicted. Accessing a key (via\n    get or put) marks it as most recently used.\n\n    Attributes:\n        capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can hold. Must be >= 1.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int) -> None:\n        if capacity < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be at least 1\")\n        self.capacity: int = capacity\n        self._cache: OrderedDict[Hashable, Any] = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key: Hashable) -> Optional[Any]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with `key`.\n\n        If the key exists, it is moved to the end of the OrderedDict to mark\n        it as most recently used, and its value is returned.\n\n        If the key does not exist, None is returned.\n\n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n\n        Returns:\n            The value if the key is present, otherwise None.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self._cache:\n            return None\n\n        # Move the key to the end to mark it as most recently used\n        self._cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self._cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key: Hashable, value: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or",
